      "latency_ms": 2340.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 518,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* Water molecules have a \"bent\" shape, which gives them a positive charge on the hydrogen side and a negative charge on the oxygen side. Because of this polarity, water molecules attract each other like tiny magnets, which is why water beads up, clings to surfaces, and dissolves many substances (earning it the title of the **\"universal solvent\"**).\n\n### 2. The Physical View\nUnder normal conditions on Earth, water is a liquid, but it is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states:\n* **Liquid:** The fluid we drink, swim in, and use for agriculture and industry.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water. Uniquely, solid water is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, which drives our weather systems and creates clouds.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Geological View\nWater covers roughly **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It is constantly moving through what we call the **water cycle** (or hydrologic cycle): evaporating from oceans, forming clouds, falling as rain or snow, and flowing through rivers back to the sea. \n\n### 4. The Biological View\nWater is the foundation of all known life. \n* The human body is made up of roughly 60% water.\n* Cells are filled with water-based fluid (cytoplasm), where all the chemical reactions of life take place.\n* Without water, living organisms cannot transport nutrients, regulate body temperature, or flush out waste. \n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, shaping our landscapes, regulating our climate, and making biological existence possible."
